---
import BaseLayout from '../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
// Results page for the AI coding-agent evaluation. Route: wheels.dev/ai
// Primary figures are the rescored ones (two over-strict hidden checks were
// corrected after the runs); raw figures are shown in parentheses throughout.
// Open items for a later pass: cover/hero art, a results date, and a
// "methods + raw data" link if the data files are published.
---

<BaseLayout
	title="Building Wheels apps with AI coding agents"
	description="We ran a set of coding agents at real feature work in a Wheels application and measured how often they finished the job. Every number, and what we changed in Wheels as a result."
	current="landing"
>
	<section class="hero">
		<div class="hero__inner">
			<p class="hero__eyebrow">AI coding agents</p>
			<h1 class="hero__title">Building Wheels apps with <span>AI coding agents</span></h1>
			<p class="hero__subtitle">
				We ran a set of coding agents at real feature work in a Wheels application and measured how
				often they finished the job. This page reports what we measured, every number, and what we
				changed in Wheels as a result.
			</p>
			<div class="stat">
				<div class="stat__figure">700 <span>/ 790</span></div>
				<div class="stat__label">
					scored trials passed — <strong>88.6%</strong>
					<span class="stat__sub">660 / 790 (83.5%) raw, before two hidden-check corrections</span>
				</div>
			</div>
		</div>
	</section>

	<article class="prose report">
		<p>
			Our aim was a <strong>like-for-like</strong> comparison with the way Ruby on Rails' own
			<a href="https://github.com/rails/ai-evals">ai-evals</a> measures agents: the same harness, the
			same agent, a comparable sample app, and the same style of task with hidden acceptance checks. Where
			we couldn't match Rails exactly, we say so.
		</p>

		<h2 id="1-what-we-measured-and-why">1. What we measured, and why</h2>
		<ul>
			<li>
				<strong>Same harness and agent as Rails' ai-evals.</strong> We used Rails'
				<code>lemans</code> harness and its <code>miniswen</code> agent unchanged, so a Wheels task is
				run and scored the same way a Rails task is.
			</li>
			<li>
				<strong>A comparable sample app.</strong> Tasks run against a Writebook-style content app built
				in Wheels — the same kind of app Rails' Stage 1 uses — so the asks line up.
			</li>
			<li>
				<strong>Frozen tasks with hidden checks.</strong> Each task is a plain-English feature request.
				The agent gets the prompt and nothing else; a hidden spec decides pass/fail. A task scores 1 only
				if the app's own test suite is green <strong>and</strong> every hidden check passes. Tasks and
				their checks were frozen and independently reviewed before any model ran. The only changes after
				any model ran were the two over-strict check corrections disclosed in section 5, each reported
				with both the before and after figures.
			</li>
			<li>
				<strong>Two arms.</strong> Each model ran twice where possible: <strong>bare</strong> (a stock
				Wheels app) and <strong>shipped docs</strong> (the same app with the AI context Wheels now ships
				— <code>.ai/</code> docs and a prebuilt <code>.mcp.json</code>). This isolates whether the
				shipped context helps.
			</li>
			<li>
				<strong>Agent-native rows reported separately.</strong> Alongside the like-for-like rows we ran
				<a href="https://www.anthropic.com/claude-code">Claude Code</a> against the same frozen tasks,
				via the "Harbor" setup. Those rows are a different agent and are always reported and labelled
				separately — never pooled with the like-for-like numbers.
			</li>
		</ul>
		<p>
			Ten tasks (plus a hello-world control) × three attempts per cell. A "cell" is one model in one
			arm. Twenty-four cells in all: nine models in both arms, four more in the shipped arm only,
			and two Claude Code cells.
		</p>
		<blockquote>
			<p>
				<strong>Integrity first.</strong> The case we wanted to test honestly is simply whether Wheels
				deserves a seat at the table. So: no benchmark-specific caching, no pre-generated answers, and
				no app state an agent wouldn't have in normal use. Every improvement we made to help agents shipped
				to <strong>every</strong> Wheels user — in <code>wheels new</code>, the docs, and the MCP
				server — not just to the test. Every run was recorded, including failures and infrastructure
				errors, and those are reported below.
			</p>
		</blockquote>

