		<h2 id="3-did-the-shipped-ai-context-help">3. Did the shipped AI context help?</h2>
		<p>For the nine models that ran in both arms, with both check corrections applied:</p>
		<div class="table-wrap">
			<table class="data">
				<thead>
					<tr><th></th><th>Bare (raw)</th><th>Shipped docs (raw)</th></tr>
				</thead>
				<tbody>
					<tr><td>Total, 9 models</td><td>256/296 (236)</td><td>255/296 (244)</td></tr>
				</tbody>
			</table>
		</div>
		<p>
			<strong>Honestly: the effect is within noise.</strong> After correcting the two over-strict hidden
			checks (section 5), the shipped docs did not produce a measurable across-the-board lift — the pooled
			difference is a single trial, in the noise at three attempts per cell. Most of the apparent raw
			gain (+8 trials) came from one of the corrected checks, which the docs arm happened to pass more
			often by chance.
		</p>
		<p>
			Where the docs did help was narrower and more plausible: a model occasionally used a 4.2 API
			it would otherwise have missed, and a couple of models took fewer steps with the docs present.
			Cost and step counts barely moved with docs either way (within about ±10% on spend, under one
			step for most models).
		</p>
		<p>
			We read this as: the strong models already know how to build a Wheels app from the stock
			conventions, and the shipped context mostly helps at the margins and on newer APIs. We'd
			rather report that plainly than overstate it.
		</p>
		<blockquote>
			<p>
				The bare/docs totals here were recomputed from the rescored data plus the erase-account
				per-trial flips, and confirmed by re-running the tally: bare 256/296, docs 255/296 (raw 236
				vs 244).
			</p>
		</blockquote>

		<h2 id="4-the-ten-tasks">4. The ten tasks</h2>
		<p>
			Seven tasks mirror a Rails ai-evals task; three are Wheels-native (no Rails counterpart). Pass
			rate is across all 24 cells, rescored.
		</p>
		<div class="table-wrap">
			<table class="data">
				<thead>
					<tr><th>Task</th><th>Rails counterpart</th><th>Difficulty</th><th>Pass</th></tr>
				</thead>
				<tbody>
					<tr
						><td
							>ar-bulk-access-grants — grant a roster of readers in one insert, notify each once,
							idempotent</td
						><td>yes</td><td>medium</td><td>71/72 (99%)</td></tr
					>
					<tr
						><td
							>ar-release-recap — keep a cached "recap" matching exactly what a publish committed</td
						><td>yes</td><td>medium</td><td>71/72 (99%)</td></tr
					>
					<tr
						><td
							>ar-compact-positions — compact hand-ordered items to a dense 1..n, trashed ones
							included</td
						><td>yes</td><td>medium</td><td>66/72 (92%)</td></tr
					>
					<tr
						><td
							>as-purge-embedded-images — free image files on delete, with reference counting and
							rollback safety</td
						><td>yes</td><td>hard</td><td>63/72 (88%)</td></tr
					>
					<tr
						><td
							>sup-legacy-conversions — find and fix every removed/changed 4.x API in an admin
							report</td
						><td>yes</td><td>medium</td><td>63/72 (88%)</td></tr
					>
					<tr
						><td
							>wn-query-not-array — a "recently edited" list, and the same list as a JSON array</td
						><td>no (Wheels-native)</td><td>easy</td><td>63/72 (88%)</td></tr
					>
					<tr
						><td>ar-erase-account — erase an account and everything only it owns, atomically</td><td
							>yes</td
						><td>hard</td><td>60/72 (83%)</td></tr
					>
					<tr
						><td
							>wn-literal-route — add print + archive pages without breaking legacy redirect links</td
						><td>no (Wheels-native)</td><td>medium</td><td>60/72 (83%)</td></tr
					>
					<tr
						><td
							>wn-reversible-trash — soft-delete books to a trash with restore and a 30-day purge</td
						><td>no (Wheels-native)</td><td>medium</td><td>57/72 (79%)</td></tr
					>
					<tr
						><td
							>hw-scoped-broadcast — live updates that reach exactly the people who can currently
							read a book</td
						><td>yes</td><td>hard</td><td>54/70 (77%)</td></tr
					>
				</tbody>
			</table>
		</div>
		<p>(<code>hello-world</code> control: 72/72.)</p>
		<p><strong>What failed, and why:</strong></p>
		<ul>
			<li>
				<strong>ar-erase-account</strong> asks for an atomic, hard delete of an account and everything
				only it owns, through a soft-delete model where the default delete hides rows rather than removing
				them. Most of the genuine misses were real model mistakes: a soft-delete-only erase that leaves
				rows behind, calling object methods on raw query rows, a missing datasource, and one route-constraint
				escaping bug (below). Two more were a documentation gap, not a model error — a raw SQL subquery
				with a literal inside <code>where=</code> threw an unhelpful error, since addressed (<a
					href="https://github.com/wheels-dev/wheels/pull/4531">#4531</a
				>). Two weaker models (GPT-5.6 Luna, Haiku 4.5) account for most of them.
			</li>
			<li>
				<strong>hw-scoped-broadcast</strong> asks the agent to add per-listener authorization to a live-update
				stream that originally had none. Excluding the two weakest models, it passes 93%; the rest is
				almost entirely those two.
			</li>
			<li>
				<strong>wn-reversible-trash</strong> and <strong>wn-literal-route</strong> are Wheels-native tasks
				about soft deletes and route declaration order; the misses are again concentrated in the two weakest
				models.
			</li>
			<li>
				<strong>Haiku 4.5</strong> is the clear floor (12/33 bare, 9/33 docs) — its failures are spread
				across most tasks, not one weakness.
			</li>
		</ul>
		<p>
			A recurring, framework-agnostic lesson showed up too: CFML strings have no backslash escapes,
			so a digit route constraint must be written <code>"\d+"</code>, not <code>"\\d+"</code> — one agent
			got this wrong and broke the app's reading URLs. (We've since made that explicit in the routing
			guide.)
		</p>

		<h2 id="5-corrections-and-deviations-in-full">5. Corrections and deviations, in full</h2>
		<p>We'd rather show the rough edges than hide them.</p>
		<ul>
			<li>
				<strong>Two hidden checks were over-strict and were corrected after the runs.</strong> Both figures
				are kept; the corrected ("rescored") one is primary.
				<ol>
					<li>
						<strong>wn-literal-route</strong> demanded every section's <em>title</em> in the print view,
						when the task asks for each section's <em>content</em> (and the app's own view renders a section
						by its text). Re-scored with that one assertion relaxed and everything else unchanged, the
						task went from 27/72 to 60/72. Of the twelve trials that don't reach that figure, six are
						genuine failures (an HTTP 301 on the print route) and six are Claude Code trials that couldn't
						be re-scored (see below).
					</li>
					<li>
						<strong>ar-erase-account</strong> treated a book as "edited by nobody else," but a fixture
						user was an administrator and the app lets administrators edit every book — so "nobody else
						can edit" was ambiguous against the app's own rule. Re-scored with that fixture user demoted
						(every other check unchanged), the task went from 53/72 to 60/72; seven trials that kept the
						book because an administrator exists now pass, and no passing trial became a failure. An eighth
						trial of the same kind — a Claude Code trial — couldn't be re-scored because its diff wasn't
						captured, so it stays as originally scored.
					</li>
				</ol>
				Each correction changed exactly one thing; every other check stayed as frozen.
			</li>
			<li>
				<strong>Provider refusals.</strong> A provider's content moderation refused two Qwen3.8-27B trials
				of one task (and its retry); those two trials are out of the denominator, reported as <code
					>/32</code
				>.
			</li>
			<li>
				<strong>Infrastructure, not the model.</strong> A duplicate launch was discarded and re-run; a
				pricing-metadata bug cost two trials at step one (fixed, re-run); a couple of transient provider
				503s were retried. Eight re-runs in all ($4.74). None changed a scored result; the originals are
				kept as evidence.
			</li>
			<li>
				<strong>One agent-native web fetch.</strong> In one Claude Code trial, the agent fetched a public
				Wheels upgrade guide page twice and solved the task. The Claude Code rows allow web access by
				design (it's an agent-native setup), so this is within that row's definition — but it's the first
				time it actually happened, so we flag it: that one task's Claude Code result isn't strictly comparable
				to the like-for-like rows.
			</li>
			<li>
				<strong>Three Claude Code trials per cell couldn't be re-scored</strong> for the corrected wn-literal-route
				check, because that agent's diff wasn't captured. They stay as originally scored.
			</li>
		</ul>

		<h2 id="6-what-changed-in-wheels-because-of-this">6. What changed in Wheels because of this</h2>
		<p>
			Per our integrity rule, everything the rounds surfaced was fixed for <strong>all</strong>
			Wheels users, not just the test. Running real agents at real tasks turned out to be an excellent
			way to find rough edges. A selection of the user-facing changes the rounds drove, each a public
			PR:
		</p>
		<ul>
			<li>
				<strong><code>savedChanges()</code> and saved-change state after a save</strong>
				(<a href="https://github.com/wheels-dev/wheels/pull/4349">#4349</a>) — so an
				<code>afterCommit</code> can tell what the commit actually wrote, instead of apps hand-keeping
				flags and getting the lifecycle wrong.
			</li>
			<li>
				<strong><code>transaction="savepoint"</code></strong> for nested transaction units (<a
					href="https://github.com/wheels-dev/wheels/pull/4040">#4040</a
				>).
			</li>
			<li>
				<strong>An application data cache</strong> — <code>appCacheFetch()</code> and friends (<a
					href="https://github.com/wheels-dev/wheels/pull/4388">#4388</a
				>).
			</li>
			<li>
				<strong>Query assertions in specs</strong> — <code>assertQueries()</code> and friends (<a
					href="https://github.com/wheels-dev/wheels/pull/4357">#4357</a
				>).
			</li>
			<li>
				<strong>A clock seam for tests</strong> — <code>travelTo()</code>
				(<a href="https://github.com/wheels-dev/wheels/pull/4387">#4387</a>) — and a test client
				that can set the request's client address (<a
					href="https://github.com/wheels-dev/wheels/pull/4343">#4343</a
				>).
			</li>
			<li>
				<strong>Forms inside cached fragments</strong> work correctly (<a
					href="https://github.com/wheels-dev/wheels/pull/4032">#4032</a
				>); production error pages return the right HTTP status (<a
					href="https://github.com/wheels-dev/wheels/pull/4238">#4238</a
				>); date comparisons in the query builder match correctly on SQLite (<a
					href="https://github.com/wheels-dev/wheels/pull/4281">#4281</a
				>); a failed background-job enqueue now raises instead of being silently dropped (<a
					href="https://github.com/wheels-dev/wheels/pull/4266">#4266</a
				>), and a job enqueued inside a datasource transaction is deferred to its commit so it runs
				predictably (<a href="https://github.com/wheels-dev/wheels/pull/4282">#4282</a>).
			</li>
			<li>
				<strong>Dozens of smaller docs, CLI, and upgrade-path fixes</strong> — a fresh-VM install-and-upgrade
				walkthrough, driven by watching agents (and new users) hit the same cliffs, produced a long run
				of corrections to <code>wheels new</code>, the upgrade commands, and the guides.
			</li>
		</ul>
		<blockquote>
			<p>
				Several of these — the application cache, query assertions, the clock seam — are new in 4.2
				precisely because an agent needed them and we agreed every Wheels developer would too.
			</p>
		</blockquote>

		<h2 id="7-how-we-kept-it-honest">7. How we kept it honest</h2>
		<ul>
			<li>
				<strong>Frozen before any model ran.</strong> Tasks and their hidden checks were fixed and independently
				reviewed before the first run. The only post-run changes were the two over-strict check corrections
				above, which relaxed over-strict checks and are reported with both figures.
			</li>
			<li>
